# <%.Name%> frontend

React + TypeScript + TanStack Router/Query with the saved **<%.UI%>** profile.
Generated features and component source belong to your application.

## Local development

Use Node 24.22.1 (mise is pinned in this directory):

```sh
mise trust
mise install
mise exec -- npm install --ignore-scripts
mise exec -- npm run api:generate
mise exec -- npm run fmt
mise exec -- npm run check
mise exec -- npm run dev
```

Start the Go API separately on http://127.0.0.1:8080. Open
https://127.0.0.1:5173 and accept its local self-signed certificate warning.
OpenSSL generates a 30-day certificate; .cert/ is ignored and its key is 0600.
No OS trust changes are made. Never reuse this certificate in production.

The HTTPS proxy preserves Origin and Secure/HttpOnly cookies. Explicitly set
`<%.Prefix%>AUTH_TRUSTED_ORIGINS=https://127.0.0.1:5173` in the API environment.
For another numeric-loopback API port set `<%.Prefix%>WEB_API_TARGET` in Vite's
process. No wildcard CORS, cookie rewriting or JS token storage is generated.

Auth UI requires the backend local-credential capability. It does not implement
credential issuance, delivery, shared limits, trusted ingress or provider setup.
Complete the backend's required integrations and migrations before using it.
Account enrollment grants no business permissions or tenant membership.
Verification/recovery responses stay generic. Tokens are pasted into a POST form,
never consumed from a URL. Auth fields and mutation values are transient memory,
not a secure-erasure guarantee.

## Add features from the project root

```sh
pluto frontend add component purchase-form --kind form
pluto frontend add component review-dialog --kind dialog
pluto frontend add auth
```

Supported component kinds: button, input, card, dialog, form. These are usable
presentation components: forms accept onSubmit, buttons accept onAction, dialogs
provide accessible open/close behavior. Wire business logic through feature hooks.
The component gallery demonstrates them without pretending to save server data.
The CLI automatically adds required primitives and dependencies for the saved UI.

Only catalog.gen.ts and manifest/package dependency metadata are updated later.
Existing feature files and UI primitives are preserved. Conflicts abort before
writes. `--dry-run` is offline and writes nothing; `--no-install` writes scaffolding
without running npm. Default installation disables lifecycle scripts, generates
API bindings, formats source and runs frontend checks. Tool failures leave the
scaffold in place and report repair instructions. Commit package-lock.json.
Changing ui in pluto.toml alone is refused; library migration is deliberate.

## Structure and checks

- app/: providers, routes, composition and generated feature catalog.
- features/: feature-owned forms/components, queries/mutations and public.ts.
- shared/ui/: application-owned shadcn source when selected; HeroUI imports its package.
- api/: the single typed transport, generated from local Go OpenAPI.

`npm run check` checks OpenAPI freshness, lint/format, import boundaries, types,
behavior tests and production build. The static architecture checker is a
convention guard, not a semantic proof or security sandbox. Source limits include
generated API modules; npm's standard lockfile is the sole text exception.
`pluto fmt` / `pluto vet` include the saved frontend after dependencies are installed.

Deploy static assets and Go behind one HTTPS origin. Configure SPA fallback away
from /api and /auth, trusted origins and a production HTML CSP. Vite's loopback
proxy/certificate setup is for local development, not a production auth deployment.
